WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:05.572
[music playing]

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.947
On the eve of Princess
Diana's funeral,

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:17.584
the royal family is returning
to London, hoping perhaps

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.953
to quell some of the
criticism of their actions

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:22.489
since Diana's death.

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:25.158
REPORTER: The queen's
convoy arrived in London.

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.327
As it swept up to
Buckingham Palace,

00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:29.262
the limousine
halted at the gates,

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.531
and the queen and Prince
Philip got out to take

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:35.034
in the massive floral tributes.

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:38.171
INGRID SEWARD: The queen was
actually extremely nervous

00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:39.973
because she could
feel the hostility,

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:41.741
and actually she
heard people say,

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:43.276
ooh, about time you've come.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:45.678
You know, they could hear
an awful lot of things

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:46.746
that the crowds say.

00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:50.283
And then I think a
woman actually gave her

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.018
a bunch of flowers
and said, "Would you

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:53.319
like me to place it for you?"

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.255
And the woman said, "No,
ma'am, it's for you."

00:00:58.525 --> 00:00:59.893
And that sort of broke the ice.

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:01.394
It was that moment.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:05.698
And I think suddenly people
saw her, instead of feeling

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:07.700
all prickly and angry,
they suddenly thought,

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.302
my goodness, this
is a grandmother,

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:11.538
and she's got to
deal with all this.

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:26.820
REPORTER: Queen
Elizabeth will speak

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:30.490
publicly today about the death
of Diana, Princess of Wales.

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:33.159
Joining me from London
is NPR's Michael Goldfarb

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:35.261
to talk about the
extraordinary move

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.397
by the queen to speak
to her subjects.

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:39.532
Any idea what she
will say today?

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:41.367
MICHAEL GOLDFARB: It
will be a very carefully

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.870
crafted speech because
people will listen

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:45.972
to it with great interest.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:48.741
Since last Sunday's
dreadful news,

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:52.512
we have seen throughout
Britain and around the world

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:57.383
an overwhelming expression
of sadness of Diana's death.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.888
So what I say to you now as
your queen and as a grandmother

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.991
I say from my heart.

00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:09.095
First, I want to pay
tribute to Diana myself.

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:12.732
She was an exceptional
and gifted human being.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:16.469
In good times and bad, she
never lost her capacity

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:20.039
to smile and laugh
nor to inspire others

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:22.976
with her warmth and kindness.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:26.913
I admired and respected her
for her energy and commitment

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.882
to others.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:32.986
I hope that tomorrow we can
all, wherever we are, join

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:37.423
in expressing our grief at
Diana's loss and gratitude

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:38.892
for her all-too-short life.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.563
I thought she said everything
she should have said.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.165
Can't think of anything
that she left out at all.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.368
It is completely appropriate.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:05.585
REPORTER: And they're
gathering now at the entrance.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:12.559
This is an extraordinary
view we're seeing here.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:14.561
We've never, as
far as I'm aware,

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:17.830
seen the royal family standing
like this at the gates

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:18.731
of Buckingham Palace.

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:30.944
INGRID SEWARD: At the funeral,
the queen was very dignified,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.348
and she bowed her head when
Diana's coffin went past.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:43.856
And it was spectacularly done.

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:51.431
QUEEN ELIZABETH II: I, for one,
believe there are lessons to be

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:55.268
drawn from her life and from
the extraordinary and moving

00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:58.304
reaction to her death.

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:01.274
It is a chance to show
to the whole world

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:05.645
the British nation united
in grief and respect.

